# booking.zd — the two typed fields, and the types that earn them a place.
#
# `Input` renders a box a reader types digits into and hands the program
# `Text`. No `Text`-to-number conversion exists in the prelude, so a
# quantity, a price or an age had no route from the field to the type the
# program computes with. These two elements are that route, and each is
# here for its type rather than for its appearance.
#
# ⚠️ BOTH BIND AN `Option`, AND THAT IS THE POINT. A box with nothing
# usable in it — empty, a lone `-`, a half-written `1e`, a date the reader
# has not finished — has no number in it, and the browser says so with
# `NaN`. `NaN` is not a value this language has: it is neither `Whole` nor
# `Decimal`, and it survives every arithmetic operation to arrive at a
# text node spelling the word `NaN`. Zero would be worse, because zero is
# a number somebody may have meant. So the empty box is `None`, and the
# `when` below is the program saying what it does about it.
#
# ⚠️ A DATE IS A MOMENT, AND THERE IS NO `Date` TYPE. `prelude/time.zd`
# already fixes what a point in time is here — a `Whole` of milliseconds
# since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, UTC, which is what `clock` gives — and
# `civilDateOf`, `weekdayOf` and `momentOf` read one apart and put it back
# together. HTML defines a date field's `valueAsNumber` as exactly that
# number, so the control and the prelude already agree and nothing in
# between formats a date. `DateInput` yields the type the language has
# rather than a new half-typed one that none of those functions accept.

state guests   is client Option of Whole starting None
state arriving is client Option of Whole starting None

view title is "Booking"
    Column padding is 16, gap is 12, maxWidth is 480

        Heading "Book a table"

        # `least`, `most` and `step` are the browser's `min`, `max` and
        # `step`. They narrow the spinner and the keyboard; they do not
        # make an out-of-range entry impossible, which is why they are
        # optional here and required on a `Slider` — a range control
        # really does clamp, and this one really does not.
        Label "How many people", controls is "guests"
        NumberInput guests, id is "guests", least is 1, most is 12, step is 1, hint is "2"

        # No `least` or `most`: a date field's bounds are ISO date
        # strings, and the shared argument table types those two names as
        # numbers. An earliest and a latest day are not expressible, and
        # saying so beats approximating them.
        Label "Which day", controls is "arriving"
        DateInput arriving, id is "arriving"

        when guests
            None
                Paragraph "Say how many are coming."
            Some with people
                # A number, not the text of one: this multiplies.
                Paragraph "Seats to lay: "
                    Strong (text of (people * 2))

        when arriving
            None
                Paragraph "Pick a day."
            Some with moment
                # The prelude's own civil calendar, applied to what the
                # reader picked. Nothing here parses a string.
                Row gap is 6
                    Text "Arriving in "
                    Strong (text of (civilDateOf of moment).year)
                    Text ", month "
                    Strong (text of (civilDateOf of moment).month)
                    Text ", day "
                    Strong (text of (civilDateOf of moment).day)
                # 0 is Sunday and 6 is Saturday, from `weekdayOf`.
                Paragraph "Weekday number: "
                    Strong (text of (weekdayOf of moment))
